    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight mb-4 text-white">
      Airless Spray Tip Sizing: The 515 vs 517 vs 413 Formula
    </h1>

    <p class="text-slate-300 text-base sm:text-lg max-w-3xl leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Choosing the wrong airless spray tip causes <strong>excessive paint fogging</strong>, heavy edge lines known as "tails", and sagging paint curtains. Mastering the two hidden numbers stamped on every reversible tip—the <strong>fan angle multiplier</strong> and the <strong>orifice diameter in thousandths of an inch</strong>—is the fundamental secret to professional exterior coatings.
    </p>

    <!-- Interactive Tip Breakdown Diagram -->
    <div class="bg-sky-950/70 border border-sky-500/30 rounded-xl p-5 max-w-3xl">
      <h3 class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-300 font-bold mb-3">How to Decode a 3-Digit Tip Number (e.g. 515)</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 text-center">
        <div class="bg-slate-950/80 p-4 rounded-lg border border-sky-500/20">
          <span class="text-3xl font-black text-sky-400">First Digit: "5"</span>
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-white mt-1">Fan Angle &times; 2 = Fan Width</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 mt-2">5 &times; 2 = <strong>10-inch fan</strong> when spraying at the standard 12-inch standoff distance from the wall.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-950/80 p-4 rounded-lg border border-sky-400/40">
          <span class="text-3xl font-black text-amber-300">Last Two Digits: "15"</span>
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-white mt-1">Orifice Hole Size in Inches</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 mt-2">0.015-inch opening. Dictates gallons per minute (0.24 GPM) and paint viscosity handling.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Main Content -->
  <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-6 mb-12">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">Comparing the Big Three: 515 vs 517 vs 413</h2>
    <p>
      For residential exterior painting, 90% of all work is completed using one of three standard tip sizes:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>515 Tip (The Gold Standard for Residential Siding):</strong> Delivers a 10-inch fan with a 0.015-inch orifice. It puts out approximately 0.24 GPM, providing the perfect balance between high production speed and manageable wet-edge control for single-operator DIY painters. Ideal for standard exterior 100% acrylic latex.</li>
      <li><strong>517 Tip (Commercial Production for Heavy Paints):</strong> Delivers a 10-inch fan with a larger 0.017-inch orifice, flowing 0.31 GPM (nearly 30% more material). Required for heavy high-build elastomeric paints or thick primers. However, it can quickly overwhelm amateur painters, leading to heavy runs and sags if hand speed hesitates.</li>
      <li><strong>413 Tip (Trim, Fascia & Soffits):</strong> Produces an 8-inch fan with a smaller 0.013-inch orifice (0.18 GPM). The narrower fan and restricted flow provide surgical control on 6-inch fascia boards, corner trim, window casings, and exterior doors with zero overspray drift.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- Comparative Table -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8">Airless Tip Engineering & Flow Rates</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto not-prose my-6">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-sm text-gray-700 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
