    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">How to Stop "Tailing" (Fingering) Without Maxing Out Pressure</h2>
    <p>
      "Tails" occur when paint sprays heavier along the top and bottom edges of the fan than in the center, leaving prominent stripes. Beginners instinctively crank their pump pressure to the maximum 3,000 PSI, which creates explosive clouds of airborne overspray that float onto neighbors' cars. The proper troubleshooting sequence:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Turn pressure up gradually:</strong> Start at low pressure (1,400 PSI) and spray test cardboard, increasing pressure until the edges soften into a smooth oval.</li>
      <li><strong>Switch to a Fine Finish Low Pressure (FFLP) tip:</strong> Modern RAC X FFLP tips atomize paint at 50% lower pressure (1,200–1,500 PSI), eliminating tails while reducing paint bounce-back overspray by up to 55%.</li>
      <li><strong>Check for tip wear:</strong> Spraying abrasive latex through carbide tips wears the orifice round. After 40 to 60 gallons, a 515 tip wears into a 417, halving fan width and doubling paint volume. When your fan narrows, replace the tip immediately.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- Trade Pro Field Note -->
  <div class="mb-10">
    <FieldNote
      title="Matching Your Tip Size to Pump Maximum GPM Rating"
      trade="Airless Equipment Engineering"
      content="Never put a 517 or 519 tip on a homeowner-grade electric pump (such as a Graco Magnum X5) that is only rated for a maximum of 0.27 GPM with a 0.015-inch max tip. The pump motor will cycle continuously, overheat, and fail to maintain consistent pressure, causing constant pressure drops and spattering. Always verify your pump's maximum tip rating before buying accessories."
    />
  </div>

